for Black History Month we are  celebrating trailblazers who became  leaders in their field and helped change  the course of history this morning we  hear from two civil rights attorneys  Fred gray was the lawyer for Rosa Parks  during the Montgomery bus boycott in  1955 and dr. Martin Luther King jr. an  Alabama native gray is now 89 years old  Benjamin Crump is a renowned attorney  known for representing the families of  the slain teenagers Trayvon Martin in  Florida and Michael Brown in Missouri  Crump who is 50 years old practices law  in five states and Washington DC Gray  has mentored Crump for more than 20  years we bought them together at the  Tuskegee History Center in Alabama to  talk about their life's work and paving  the way for countless others in the  modern civil rights movement well when I  was growing up in Montgomery and the  third is fathers Fisher's that were  basically only two possessions that  [Music]  african-american boys could really look  at as being professions that people  thought a lot about but we did have  teachers and preachers and I kind of  decided that I would probably try to be  both my father died when I was two my  mother was a domestic worker only went  to the fourth grade but she told the  five of us as we grew up in the west  side of Montgomery that we could be  anything we wanted to be if we did three  things one came Christ first in your  life  second stay in schools and got a good  education and three stay out of trouble  I tried to do all  in 1947 I enrolled in Alabama State  College for Negroes in Montgomery living  on the west side of town and the school  was on the east side of town so I used  the buses all the time for that purpose  by using the buses I observed that our  people were mistreated we were  frequently asked to get up and give our  seats to white persons and I observed  that everything was completely  segregated and we had a family friend  named edy Nixon who was a very  interested in civil rights and had been  president of the Montgomery chapter the  NAACP  he encouraged me to become a lawyer I  made a personal secret commitment in my  upper teen age that I was going to  finish law school come back to Alabama  take the bar exam become a lawyer and  destroy everything segregated I could  find  I wanted to be a lawyer ever since I was  in the fourth grade much like my mentor  Fred gray story is certain things that  trigger you to say you want to go be a  champion for justice and opponent to  enemies of equality and for me that was  when I was in the fourth grade  growing up in Lumberton North Carolina  our deliberate speed from the Supreme  Court decision and Brown didn't get to  Lumberton until around the late 70s and  so in 1979 I was in the fourth grade and  that's when they best black children  from South Flemington across the tracks  to the school in the white community  that was a newer school with newer books  and newer technology and all the black  children got free lunch and it would be  a long line of us waiting and a little  white children they could go get ala  carte lunches and they could get  cheeseburgers and Pete said french fries  everything that little children want and  I kept thinking to myself man why is it  that some people in certain parts of the  community have it so good  and others seem to have it so  challenging in my community and I  remember my mother told me it was  because of Brown versus the Board  Education and Thurgood Marshall and the  n-double-a-cp lawyers while we got to go  to the school with the new books and the  new technology and new equipment and I  said to myself I'm gonna be like they're  gonna march in the n-double-a-cp lawyers  when I grow up because I want to make it  better for people and my community  people who look like me and from that  day until this one that's what I've been  striving to do so I always knew that I  wanted to be a lawyer and fight for the  least of these as my grandmother's say  the people who need a voice the people  who are often marginalized  disenfranchised and dehumanized we are  amazed I am at your recall I mean I pray  Fred when I get to be 89 years old that  I can recall dates and facts like you're  doing that I mean well I didn't have the  privilege of going to enter integrated  anything yes sir and my whole idea was  to destroy segregation I started going  to the NAACP meetings and I met Rosa  Parks we would sit and share our lunches  and talk about the problems so on  December 1st it was like any other day  that we had talked we had our lunch came  back late in the evening and I had phone  calls from a whole lot of people and I  found out that miss parks had been  arrested  I went over she told me she wanted me to  represent her and I told I would but I  also told us several miss parks you know  we've been dealing with this problem on  the buses for a long time know  but you know there has been talk in the  community about people staying off of  the buses as a protest and if we're  gonna do that now is the time to make a  long story short went crosstown to join  robbing sums house and we sat in her  living room on the evening of December  1st in the morning of December 2nd and  made the plans for the Montgomery bus  boycott but then we said that we need to  have the preachers involved in it a  majority of the Negro citizens of  Montgomery Alabama have refrain from  riding city buses since December 5th  that's how dr. King was introduced and  then officially dr. King was made and  appointed the spokesman for the group  and the rest of it is history outrage  over the fatal shooting of  seventeen-year-old Trayvon Martin  protesters who are demanding the arrest  of that neighborhood watch captain I  think a lot went into the background of  people knowing about Trayvon Martin and  this kid who was walking home minding  his business when this neighborhood  watch volunteer with a 9-millimeter gun  profile pursued and shot him in the  heart two days later his father called  I cannot articulate this sound that was  coming from his voice this sound of  heart brokenness he said my son Trayvon  Martin  was walking home from the 7-eleven and  the neighborhood watch volunteer shot  and killed him there are two battles is  the bout in the court of law and about  in the court of public opinion no  justice and I understood that the only  way we were gonna win this bout in the  court of public opinion there's gonna be  with the young people in the young  energy and those young people who will  become black lives matter it was the  young people on social media who got  Trayvon Martin to mainstream media and  it was a lesson now that has been  duplicated dozens and dozens of times  since then  [Music]  frequently people will say well Fred  gray they say you did so much in the  silver rights movement I never did see  you out there marching I said that's  correct  I'm only moist for ceremonial reasons my  role was not to March my role was to get  the law and held the law protect the  individuals so they could March the  civil rights march consisted of people  doing a lot of things so I made speeches  some marched some did legal work but it  took all of it but it was because we  worked together and was able to do it  but the struggle continues our ways  marvel at what dr. King said when he  said we all have a role to play in the  struggle  [Music]  you
 

